<%# Design preview: should a teacher see the split by reason, and where does it go?

    The open question in design-todo.md. Rendered with the app's real table classes, card surface and
    button variants, so what is on screen is what it would look like shipped - not a drawing of it.

    Every figure is computed from GradeEntry's own constants, so no rate here can claim something the
    model does not pay. Improvement earnings are left out of the arithmetic on purpose: they need last
    quarter's entries, and a preview that invented them would be inventing money. %>
<% content_for :own_heading, true %>

<% rows = [
     { name: "Ava Mitchell",    username: "ava_m",   math: "A", reading: "B", days: 42, perfect: true },
     { name: "Noah Brennan",    username: "noah_b",  math: "B", reading: "A", days: 40, perfect: false },
     { name: "Sofia Delgado",   username: "sofia_d", math: "A", reading: "A", days: 43, perfect: true },
     { name: "Elijah Foster",   username: "elijah",  math: "C", reading: "B", days: 38, perfect: false }
   ] %>

<% grade_pay = lambda do |grade|
     case grade
     when "A" then GradeEntry::EARNINGS_FOR_A_GRADE
     when "B" then GradeEntry::EARNINGS_FOR_B_GRADE
     else 0
     end
   end %>

<%# The money keys are named apart from the grade keys on purpose: merging `math:` over a row whose
    `math` is "A" replaced the letter with 300, and the Math column printed cents. %>
<% rows = rows.map do |row|
     attendance = (row[:days] * GradeEntry::EARNINGS_PER_DAY_ATTENDANCE) +
                  (row[:perfect] ? GradeEntry::EARNINGS_FOR_PERFECT_ATTENDANCE : 0)
     math_pay = grade_pay.call(row[:math])
     reading_pay = grade_pay.call(row[:reading])
     row.merge(
       attendance_pay: attendance,
       math_pay:,
       reading_pay:,
       total: attendance + math_pay + reading_pay
     )
   end %>

<% totals = { attendance: rows.sum { it[:attendance_pay] },
              math: rows.sum { it[:math_pay] },
              reading: rows.sum { it[:reading_pay] } } %>
<% grand_total = totals.values.sum %>
<% money = ->(cents) { number_to_currency(cents / 100.0) } %>

<%# The rows are identical in every preview, so they are a partial-shaped local block rather than four
    copies that can drift apart while I am editing one of them. %>
<% grade_rows = capture do %>
  <% rows.each do |row| %>
    <tr class="table-body-row">
      <td class="table-body-cell">
        <span class="block font-medium text-slate-900"><%= row[:name] %></span>
        <span class="block text-xs text-slate-600"><%= row[:username] %></span>
      </td>
      <td class="table-body-cell"><%= row[:math] %></td>
      <td class="table-body-cell"><%= row[:reading] %></td>
      <td class="table-body-cell"><%= row[:days] %></td>
      <td class="table-body-cell"><%= row[:perfect] ? "Yes" : "No" %></td>
      <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right"><%= money.call(row[:total]) %></td>
    </tr>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<% head = capture do %>
  <thead>
    <tr class="table-header-row">
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Student</th>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Math</th>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Reading</th>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Days attended</th>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Perfect attendance</th>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right">Earns</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
<% end %>

<%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
           locals: { title: "Who sees the earnings split",
                     description: "Four ways to answer one question, as a teacher would see it" } do %><% end %>

<%= render "components/ui/callout",
           tone: :info,
           title: "This is a preview, not shipped UI",
           body: "Development only, and nothing here is wired to data. The figures come from " \
                 "GradeEntry's rates, so they are arithmetically real and the students are not. " \
                 "The question and its constraints are recorded in design-todo.md, so this page " \
                 "can be deleted without losing the decision." %>

<div class="mt-6 space-y-10">
  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- A: today %>
  <section aria-labelledby="option-a">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="option-a" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        A. What a teacher sees today
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Every row's own figure in Earns, and the class total in the table's footer. The split into
        attendance, math and reading is on the finalize block, which only an admin sees.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table-base">
          <%= head %>
          <tbody class="bg-white"><%= grade_rows %></tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr class="table-body-row">
              <th scope="row" class="table-body-cell text-left font-medium text-slate-900" colspan="5">
                Total for <%= pluralize(rows.size, "student") %>
              </th>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right font-semibold text-slate-900">
                <%= money.call(grand_total) %>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="tw-btn-primary" disabled>Save grades</button>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      The teacher can see what the quarter pays and what each student earns. What they cannot see is
      how the payment divides - so they cannot tell that reading is paying
      <%= money.call(totals[:reading]) %> against attendance's <%= money.call(totals[:attendance]) %>
      without adding it up by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- B: recommended %>
  <section aria-labelledby="option-b">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="option-b" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        B. The whole summary moves out of the grid, into an invoice totals block
        <span class="ml-1 align-middle"><%= render "components/ui/badge", label: "Recommended", tone: :success %></span>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        The table's footer goes, and the components plus the total sit under it in one block, aligned
        to the Earns column. This is the shape Stripe, QuickBooks and Xero use: line items, then a
        narrow right-aligned stack of subtotals and the total. One place to look, and nothing pretends
        to be a table row.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="table-base">
            <%= head %>
            <tbody class="bg-white"><%= grade_rows %></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <%# Outside the horizontal scroller, inside the card. That is the whole implementation
            problem noted in design-todo.md: shared/_table_container puts the surface and the
            scroller on one element, so this needs a footer slot on the container. %>
        <div class="border-t border-slate-200 px-4 py-3">
          <dl class="ml-auto max-w-xs space-y-1 text-sm">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
              <dt class="text-slate-600">Attendance, including bonuses</dt>
              <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:attendance]) %></dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
              <dt class="text-slate-600">Math</dt>
              <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:math]) %></dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
              <dt class="text-slate-600">Reading</dt>
              <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:reading]) %></dd>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border-t border-slate-200 pt-2">
              <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Total for <%= pluralize(rows.size, "student") %></dt>
              <dd class="font-semibold tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(grand_total) %></dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="tw-btn-primary" disabled>Save grades</button>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      Costs: the totals leave the <code class="text-xs">&lt;tfoot&gt;</code>, so a screen reader no
      longer finds them as part of the grid - they become a description list after it, which is what an
      invoice does and is still correctly labelled. And it needs the footer slot on
      <code class="text-xs">shared/_table_container</code> described in design-todo.md, because the card
      surface and the horizontal scroller are the same element today.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C: both %>
  <section aria-labelledby="option-c">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="option-c" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        C. Keep the footer total, add the components below it
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        The column summary stays where a screen reader expects it and the split sits under the card,
        on the page background. Cheapest to build - no change to the shared table container.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table-base">
          <%= head %>
          <tbody class="bg-white"><%= grade_rows %></tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr class="table-body-row">
              <th scope="row" class="table-body-cell text-left font-medium text-slate-900" colspan="5">
                Total for <%= pluralize(rows.size, "student") %>
              </th>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right font-semibold text-slate-900">
                <%= money.call(grand_total) %>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <dl class="-mt-2 mb-6 ml-auto max-w-xs space-y-1 pr-4 text-sm">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-slate-600">Attendance, including bonuses</dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:attendance]) %></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-slate-600">Math</dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:math]) %></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-slate-600">Reading</dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:reading]) %></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <button type="button" class="tw-btn-primary" disabled>Save grades</button>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      Why I would not pick it: two summaries of one column, eight inches apart, and the eye has to
      decide which one is the answer. The total is inside the card and its own components are outside
      it, which says they are separate things when they are the same arithmetic.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- D: not these %>
  <section aria-labelledby="option-d">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="option-d" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        D. Two shapes already rejected, kept here so they are a decision and not an omission
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div class="tw-card p-5">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">Extra rows in the table's footer</p>
        <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
          Built and reported as unreadable: "difficult to read as it's part of the columns, but not
          actually under the titles of the columns". A row in a grid claims the headers above it
          describe it, and these labels span five columns that describe none of them.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 opacity-75">
          <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="table-base">
              <%= head %>
              <tbody class="bg-white"><%= grade_rows %></tbody>
              <tfoot>
                <% [["Attendance, including bonuses", totals[:attendance]], ["Math", totals[:math]],
                    ["Reading", totals[:reading]]].each do |label, cents| %>
                  <tr class="table-body-row">
                    <th scope="row" class="table-body-cell text-left font-normal text-slate-600" colspan="5">
                      <%= label %>
                    </th>
                    <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right text-slate-600"><%= money.call(cents) %></td>
                  </tr>
                <% end %>
                <tr class="table-body-row">
                  <th scope="row" class="table-body-cell text-left font-medium text-slate-900" colspan="5">
                    Total for <%= pluralize(rows.size, "student") %>
                  </th>
                  <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right font-semibold text-slate-900">
                    <%= money.call(grand_total) %>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tfoot>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tw-card p-5">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">A summary card after the save button</p>
        <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
          The shape before that one, also reported: a card below "Save grades" reads as something the
          save produced, and nothing on it says whether it counted what has just been typed. It was
          also a sixth surface on a page that needed fewer.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 opacity-75">
          <button type="button" class="tw-btn-primary" disabled>Save grades</button>

          <div class="tw-card p-5">
            <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">What this quarter pays</p>
            <dl class="mt-3 space-y-1 text-sm">
              <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-slate-600">Attendance, including bonuses</dt>
                <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:attendance]) %></dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-slate-600">Math</dt>
                <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:math]) %></dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-slate-600">Reading</dt>
                <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= money.call(totals[:reading]) %></dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- recommendation %>
  <section class="tw-card p-5" aria-labelledby="verdict">
    <h2 id="verdict" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">My recommendation</h2>

    <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Verdict</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          B, and only if the answer to the question is yes. The teacher enters every number that
          produces these figures, and design.md's rule is to gate the action rather than the
          information - which is the rule that pulled the total out of the admin block already. If it
          moves, it moves off the finalize block, which keeps naming the total in its sentence.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">The case for changing nothing</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          The split is what the payment is made of, not what the grades add up to, and it is read once
          - by whoever authorises the money. A teacher already has every figure the grades produce.
          Leaving it is defensible, and it costs nothing.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">What it needs from you</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          One answer: does a teacher need to see how the payment divides, or only what it comes to?
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Effort</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          B is about half a day, most of it the footer slot on
          <code class="text-xs">shared/_table_container</code> and re-measuring the ~20 tables that
          partial renders. C is an hour. Both are smaller than this preview.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
